  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 145 PM MDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nWe had an MCV over the area this morning, which provided some cloud \ncover and light rain across a good portion of the area. The cloud \ncover and rain has kept temperatures in our area quite a bit cooler \nthan surrounding areas (as of 1PM a station in northern Weld County \nwas still at 75 degrees). The cooler temperatures and cloud cover\nwill limit instability in portions of our area for this afternoon.\nThe heavier cloud cover is pushing to the northeast, with good\nclearing in the urban corridor. Showers and thunderstorms are \nexpected to form this afternoon across the higher terrain, pushing\neast into plains during the evening hours. A few of the storms \nthis evening could still be strong to marginally severe, but at \nthis point the main hazard looks to be heavy rainfall leading to \nflash flooding, with a secondary hazard of strong winds. \n\nThe rain chances and flooding threat will continue into Tuesday and \nWednesday. The upper level set up will remain similar, with a weak \ndisturbance or two moving through the upper level ridge aloft each \nafternoon. At the surface, PWs are forecast to remain around 150%\nto 200% of normal. The flooding threat is expected to increase \nfor tomorrow, as the warm cloud layer depth increase slightly and \nrainfall from today makes soils more prone to flooding. The \ncoverage tomorrow also looks a bit higher, with potential for a \nfew strong storms with heavier rainfall rates compared to today. \nInstability and shear will be decent enough for a few marginally \nsevere storms as well. The main hazards tomorrow will likely be \nstrong winds and heavy rain. The one limiting factor for heavy\nrain potential will be the cloud layer winds increasing a few \nknots for Tuesday, which could help storms move slightly faster. \n\nA similar set up will exist for Wednesday as well. The flooding \npotential for Wednesday will depend heavily on how much rain falls\ntoday and tomorrow. Areas that see heavy rain on today and Tuesday\nwill be more prone to flash flooding on Wednesday. Storm motions \nremain on the elevated side for Wednesday, but the high moisture \nand previous rainfall will lead to a 3rd day of flash flooding \npotential. While precipitation is not guaranteed each day at every\nlocation, there is a high chance (>70%) that over the next 3 days\nmany locations in our area will see at least 0.25 inches of rain,\nwith a good chance that a few localized areas see multiple inches\nof rain. Right now, the best chance for these higher rain amounts\nare in our eastern plains counties.\n\nThe upper level ridge will begin to shift more to the west for \nThursday into the weekend, putting us in northwest flow aloft and \ndecreasing the moisture over the area. This will significantly \nreduced our rain chances for the plains, with the potential for some \nisolated to scattered activity lingering in the higher terrain. The \nthermal ridge will remain off to our west through the work week, \nkeeping temperatures in the low to mid 90s for much of the plains. \nThe thermal ridge will start to shift more eastward by the end of \nthe week as the upper level ridge starts to tilt and push more \nnortheast.",
